  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 9, $j$3.1 Functions and Function Notation$j$, $j${"body": "A relation pairs inputs (x) with outputs (y). A function is a relation in which each input has exactly one output. The domain is the set of inputs and the range is the set of outputs.\n\nVertical line test: if any vertical line crosses a graph more than once, it is not a function.\n\nFunction notation: f(x) is read \"f of x\" and means the output when the input is x. If f(x) = 3x − 2, then f(4) = 3(4) − 2 = 10 and f(−1) = 3(−1) − 2 = −5.\n\nWorked example: {(1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 6)} is a function (two inputs may share an output). {(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4)} is not (the input 1 has two outputs).\n\nThink it through: picture a vending machine. Press one button and you get exactly one item. That is a function. If one button could give two different items, you could not predict the result, and that is not a function. Domain is what you may put in, and range is what comes out. A common mistake is reading f(x) as f times x. It means the output when the input is x. Another is squaring a negative incorrectly. For g(x) = x² + 1, g(−3) = (−3)² + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10. Use parentheses around negative inputs every time.\n\nYour task: (a) Is {(0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 3)} a function? (b) Find f(5) if f(x) = 2x + 7. (c) Find g(−3) if g(x) = x² + 1. (d) State the domain and range of {(2, 4), (3, 5), (5, 9)}.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.F.1.1, MA.912.F.1.2.", "video": "", "script": "Welcome to our unit on functions. A function is one of the most important ideas in all of mathematics, and it is simpler than it sounds. A function is a rule that takes an input and gives exactly one output.\n\nThink of a vending machine. You press one button, and you get one item. You never press one button and receive two different items. That is the idea of a function: each input has exactly one output.\n\nWe describe functions with ordered pairs, tables, graphs, or equations. The set of all possible inputs is called the domain. The set of all outputs is called the range.\n\nLet us test some sets of ordered pairs. The set one comma five, two comma five, three comma six. The inputs are one, two and three, and each input appears once. So it is a function. Notice that two different inputs can share the same output. That is allowed. Now the set one comma two, one comma three, two comma four. The input one has two different outputs, two and three. So this is not a function.\n\nFor graphs, we use the vertical line test. Imagine sliding a vertical line across the graph. If the line ever touches the graph at two points, then one input has two outputs, and the graph is not a function. A circle fails the test. A straight slanted line passes.\n\nNow function notation. Instead of writing y equals three x minus two, we write f of x equals three x minus two. This is read, f of x. It does not mean f times x. It means the output of the function named f when the input is x. To find f of four, replace x with four. Three times four is twelve, minus two is ten. So f of four equals ten. To find f of negative one, three times negative one is negative three, minus two is negative five.\n\nFunction notation is useful because it tells us both the input and the output at once. When we write f of four equals ten, we know that an input of four produces an output of ten.\n\nAnother example. If g of x equals x squared plus one, then g of negative three is negative three squared plus one. Negative three squared is nine, so the answer is ten. Be careful: the square applies to the negative sign too, because the negative three is inside the parentheses.\n\nYour task today is to decide whether a set is a function, evaluate two functions, and state a domain and range. Then take the four question check.\n\nRemember, a function is a promise of consistency. Each input leads to one result. In the same way, we serve a God who is the same yesterday, today, and for ever. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.F.1.1, MA.912.F.1.2"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.1 Functions and Function Notation$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 10, $j$3.2 Slope and Rate of Change$j$, $j${"body": "Slope measures steepness and is the rate of change: m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), which is rise over run.\n\nWorked example: through (1, 2) and (4, 11): m = (11 − 2)/(4 − 1) = 9/3 = 3.\nPositive slope rises left to right. Negative slope falls. A horizontal line has slope 0. A vertical line has undefined slope.\n\nTo graph y = mx + b: plot the y intercept (0, b), then use rise over run to find another point.\n\nAverage rate of change: temperature rising from 50°F to 74°F over 6 hours is (74 − 50)/6 = 4 degrees per hour.\n\nThink it through: slope answers the question, how much does y change when x increases by one? Always subtract in the same order on top and bottom. For (1, 5) and (4, −1), m = (−1 − 5)/(4 − 1) = −6/3 = −2. Mixing the order flips the sign and gives the wrong answer. A rate of change always has units. A car going 90 miles in 1.5 hours has a slope of 60 miles per hour. A horizontal line has slope 0, because nothing changes. A vertical line has undefined slope, because the run is 0 and you cannot divide by 0.\n\nYour task: find the slope through (a) (2, 3) and (6, 11) (b) (1, 5) and (4, −1). (c) What is the slope of y = 7? (d) A car travels 90 miles in 1.5 hours. Find the average rate in miles per hour.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.F.1.3, MA.912.AR.2.4.", "video": "", "script": "Have you ever walked up a steep hill and then a gentle one? The difference you feel is slope. In mathematics, slope measures how steep a line is. It also tells us the rate of change, how fast one quantity changes compared to another.\n\nSlope is defined as rise over run. The rise is the vertical change between two points, and the run is the horizontal change. In symbols, slope m equals y two minus y one, all divided by x two minus x one.\n\nLet us compute a slope. Take the points one comma two and four comma eleven. The rise is eleven minus two, which is nine. The run is four minus one, which is three. Nine divided by three is three. The slope is three. This means that for every one unit we move right, the line goes up three units.\n\nNow the points one comma five and four comma negative one. The rise is negative one minus five, which is negative six. The run is four minus one, which is three. Negative six divided by three is negative two. A negative slope means the line goes down as we move right.\n\nThere are two special cases. A horizontal line has no rise, so its slope is zero. A vertical line has no run, so we would divide by zero, and we say the slope is undefined. Remember: zero slope is flat, and undefined slope is straight up and down.\n\nTo graph a line from the equation y equals m x plus b, plot the y intercept first, which is the point zero comma b. Then use the slope as rise over run to find another point, and draw the line through both.\n\nIn real life, slope is a rate. Suppose the temperature rises from fifty degrees to seventy four degrees over six hours. The average rate of change is twenty four degrees divided by six hours, which is four degrees per hour. Notice we always include units when we describe a rate.\n\nHere is an important tip. Always subtract the coordinates in the same order. If you take y two minus y one on top, you must take x two minus x one on the bottom. Mixing the order changes the sign of the answer.\n\nYour task today is to find three slopes and one real world rate. Then take the four question check.\n\nRate of change shows us that progress is measured over time. Even small steady gains add up, just as Scripture says, he that gathereth by labour shall increase. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.F.1.3, MA.912.AR.2.4"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.2 Slope and Rate of Change$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 11, $j$3.3 Writing Linear Equations$j$, $j${"body": "Slope intercept form: y = mx + b (m is the slope, b is the y intercept).\nPoint slope form: y − y₁ = m(x − x₁).\nStandard form: Ax + By = C.\n\nWorked example: slope 3 through (2, 5): y − 5 = 3(x − 2), so y = 3x − 1.\nThrough (0, 1) and (2, 5): m = (5 − 1)/(2 − 0) = 2, so y = 2x + 1.\n\nParallel lines have the same slope. Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals: the line perpendicular to slope 2 has slope −1/2.\n\nWorked example: through (0, 4) parallel to y = 2x + 1 gives y = 2x + 4. Perpendicular gives y = −½x + 4.\n\nThink it through: choose the form that fits what you are given. If you know the slope and the y intercept, use y = mx + b. If you know a point and the slope, use point slope form. If you know two points, find the slope first, then use either point. Always check your line by plugging both points into the final equation. For the line through (0, 1) and (2, 5), we found y = 2x + 1. Check: 2(2) + 1 = 5, which matches the second point. Remember that perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals, so 2 and −1/2 go together.\n\nYour task: write the equation of (a) slope 3 and y intercept −2 (b) the line through (0, 1) and (2, 5) (c) the line through (0, 7) parallel to y = 4x − 3 (d) the slope of a line perpendicular to y = 2x + 5.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.2.2, MA.912.AR.2.3, MA.912.F.1.5.", "video": "", "script": "In the last lesson we found the slope of a line from two points. Today we turn that information into an equation. There are three main forms for the equation of a line, and each one is useful.\n\nThe first form is slope intercept form: y equals m x plus b. Here m is the slope and b is the y intercept, the place where the line crosses the y axis. If a line has slope three and y intercept negative two, the equation is y equals three x minus two. It is immediate.\n\nThe second form is point slope form: y minus y one equals m times the quantity x minus x one. This is the best tool when you know the slope and one point, but not the intercept. Suppose the slope is three and the line passes through two comma five. Substitute: y minus five equals three times the quantity x minus two. Distribute: y minus five equals three x minus six. Add five to both sides, and we get y equals three x minus one.\n\nWhat if we have two points but no slope? First find the slope. Take zero comma one and two comma five. The rise is four and the run is two, so the slope is two. The y intercept is given by the first point, one. The equation is y equals two x plus one.\n\nThe third form is standard form: A x plus B y equals C. It is often used for problems with two quantities, like tickets and prices.\n\nNow parallel and perpendicular lines. Parallel lines never meet, and they have the same slope. A line parallel to y equals four x minus three, passing through zero comma seven, has slope four and intercept seven. So it is y equals four x plus seven.\n\nPerpendicular lines meet at a right angle. Their slopes are negative reciprocals: flip the fraction and change the sign. If one slope is two, the perpendicular slope is negative one half. If one slope is negative three fourths, the perpendicular slope is four thirds.\n\nA good strategy is to ask yourself what information you are given. A slope and a y intercept? Use slope intercept. A slope and a point? Use point slope. Two points? Find the slope first.\n\nYour task today is to write four equations of lines, including a parallel line and a perpendicular slope. Then take the four question check.\n\nKnowing which tool to use is wisdom, and wisdom is the principal thing, as the Proverbs say. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.2.2, MA.912.AR.2.3, MA.912.F.1.5"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.3 Writing Linear Equations$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 12, $j$3.4 Linear Models in the Real World$j$, $j${"body": "A linear model describes a situation with a constant rate of change. The slope is the rate and the y intercept is the starting value.\n\nWorked example: Savings start at $120 and grow by $15 each week. y = 15x + 120. After 10 weeks: 15(10) + 120 = $270. When does it reach $420? 15x + 120 = 420 gives x = 20 weeks.\n\nConstraints: x cannot be negative, and if x counts whole weeks or items, it must be a whole number. This limits the domain.\n\nCompare two plans: Plan A costs 30 + 10g and Plan B costs 50 + 5g for g gigabytes. They are equal when 30 + 10g = 50 + 5g, so g = 4.\n\nThink it through: two clues in a word problem point to the slope and the intercept. The word per, as in per week or per gigabyte, signals the slope. A starting amount or a flat fee signals the y intercept. In y = 15x + 120, the 15 means 15 dollars saved each week, and the 120 means 120 dollars at the start. Always explain your answer with units and context. Also ask what values make sense. You cannot sell negative tickets, so the domain starts at 0 and counts whole numbers. When two plans are compared, set them equal to find the point where their costs match.\n\nYour task: (a) A phone plan costs $30 plus $10 per GB. Write the model and find the cost for 4 GB. (b) In y = 15x + 120, say what 15 and 120 mean. (c) State a sensible domain for tickets sold. (d) Compare two plans of your own.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.2.5, MA.912.F.1.5.", "video": "", "script": "Today we use linear equations as models, descriptions of real situations. A linear model has a constant rate of change. That means something grows or shrinks by the same amount each time.\n\nEvery linear model has two key parts. The slope is the rate of change, how much the output changes for each one unit of input. The y intercept is the starting value, the output when the input is zero.\n\nSuppose you start with one hundred twenty dollars in savings and add fifteen dollars each week. Let x be the number of weeks and y be the total savings. The model is y equals fifteen x plus one hundred twenty. The slope, fifteen, is the weekly savings. The y intercept, one hundred twenty, is the starting amount.\n\nWe can answer questions with this model. After ten weeks, y equals fifteen times ten plus one hundred twenty, which is two hundred seventy dollars. To find when savings reach four hundred twenty dollars, set fifteen x plus one hundred twenty equal to four hundred twenty. Subtract one hundred twenty to get fifteen x equals three hundred. Divide by fifteen, and x equals twenty. It takes twenty weeks.\n\nNow an important idea: constraints. In math class, a line goes on forever in both directions. In real life, the situation has limits. You cannot sell a negative number of tickets, so x must be zero or greater. You cannot sell half a ticket, so x must be a whole number. These limits make up the domain of the model. A good answer always checks whether the solution makes sense in context.\n\nModels also let us compare. Plan A costs thirty dollars plus ten dollars per gigabyte. Plan B costs fifty dollars plus five dollars per gigabyte. When are they equal? Set thirty plus ten g equal to fifty plus five g. Subtract five g and thirty from both sides, and we get five g equals twenty, so g equals four. At four gigabytes, both plans cost seventy dollars. For less data, Plan A is cheaper. For more data, Plan B is cheaper.\n\nYour task today is to write one model, interpret slope and intercept, state a domain, and compare two plans. Then take the four question check.\n\nGood stewardship means using numbers to plan. As Luke records, which of you, intending to build a tower, sitteth not down first, and counteth the cost? Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.2.5, MA.912.F.1.5"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.4 Linear Models in the Real World$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 3, $j$Assessment 3: Functions and Linear Models, Show Your Work$j$, $j${"prompt": "Show every step. (1) Is {(1, 4), (2, 4), (3, 7)} a function? Explain. (2) If f(x) = 2x² − x, find f(−2). (3) Find the slope through (−1, 4) and (3, −4). (4) Write the equation of the line through (2, 7) with slope 3. (5) Write the equation of the line through (0, 5) perpendicular to y = ½x − 1. (6) A youth group washes cars. They start with $50 and earn $8 per car. Write a model, find the money after 12 cars, state a sensible domain, and tell how many cars give $250.", "rubric": "Total 100. Items 1 to 5 are worth 14 each (answer 10, work 4). Item 6 is worth 30: model y = 8x + 50 (8), 12 cars gives $146 (6), domain whole numbers x ≥ 0 (6), 25 cars (6), clear sentences (4)."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 3: Functions and Linear Models, Show Your Work$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 3, $j$Discussion 3: A Rate in My Life$j$, $j${"prompt": "Describe one rate of change in your life or ministry, such as miles per hour, dollars per week, or members per month. Identify the starting value and the rate, and write a linear model. Respond in 100 to 150 words."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 3: A Rate in My Life$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 11, $j$Check 3.1: Functions and Function Notation$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Which set of ordered pairs is a function?", "options": ["{(4, 1), (4, 2), (5, 3)}", "{(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4)}", "{(1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 6)}", "{(0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3)}"]}, {"q": "If f(x) = 3x − 2, what is f(4)?", "options": ["7", "14", "12", "10"]}, {"q": "If g(x) = x² + 1, what is g(−3)?", "options": ["10", "8", "−10", "−8"]}, {"q": "What is the domain of {(2, 4), (3, 5), (5, 9)}?", "options": ["{2, 4, 5}", "{2, 3, 5}", "{3, 9}", "{4, 5, 9}"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.1: Functions and Function Notation$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,3,0,1}'::int[], array[$j$Each input appears only once, so it is a function.$j$,$j$3(4) − 2 = 10.$j$,$j$(−3)² + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10.$j$,$j$The domain is the set of inputs.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.1: Functions and Function Notation$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 12, $j$Check 3.2: Slope and Rate of Change$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Find the slope of the line through (2, 3) and (6, 11).", "options": ["8", "4", "1/2", "2"]}, {"q": "Find the slope of the line through (1, 5) and (4, −1).", "options": ["−2", "−6", "2", "−1/2"]}, {"q": "What is the slope of a horizontal line?", "options": ["0", "Undefined", "1", "−1"]}, {"q": "The temperature rises from 50°F to 74°F in 6 hours. What is the average rate of change?", "options": ["4 degrees per hour", "3 degrees per hour", "12 degrees per hour", "24 degrees per hour"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.2: Slope and Rate of Change$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,0,0,0}'::int[], array[$j$(11 − 3)/(6 − 2) = 8/4 = 2.$j$,$j$(−1 − 5)/(4 − 1) = −6/3 = −2.$j$,$j$A horizontal line has no rise, so its slope is 0.$j$,$j$24 ÷ 6 = 4 degrees per hour.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.2: Slope and Rate of Change$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 13, $j$Check 3.3: Writing Linear Equations$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Write the equation of the line with slope 3 and y intercept −2.", "options": ["y = 3x − 2", "y = x/3 − 2", "y = 3x + 2", "y = −2x + 3"]}, {"q": "Which equation is the line through (0, 1) and (2, 5)?", "options": ["y = 4x + 1", "y = 2x + 5", "y = x + 1", "y = 2x + 1"]}, {"q": "A line is parallel to y = 4x − 3 and passes through (0, 7). Its equation is:", "options": ["y = 7x + 4", "y = 4x − 3", "y = 4x + 7", "y = −¼x + 7"]}, {"q": "What is the slope of a line perpendicular to y = 2x + 5?", "options": ["2", "−1/2", "1/2", "−2"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.3: Writing Linear Equations$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,3,2,1}'::int[], array[$j$Use y = mx + b.$j$,$j$The slope is 4/2 = 2 and the y intercept is 1.$j$,$j$Parallel lines share a slope of 4.$j$,$j$Take the negative reciprocal of 2.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.3: Writing Linear Equations$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 14, $j$Check 3.4: Linear Models in the Real World$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A phone plan costs $30 plus $10 per GB. What is the cost for 4 GB?", "options": ["$70", "$120", "$40", "$34"]}, {"q": "Savings are modeled by y = 15x + 120, where x is weeks. When will savings reach $420?", "options": ["28 weeks", "36 weeks", "20 weeks", "8 weeks"]}, {"q": "In y = 15x + 120, what does the 15 represent?", "options": ["The amount saved each week", "The total savings", "The starting savings", "The number of weeks"]}, {"q": "Why is a sensible domain for tickets sold x ≥ 0, with x a whole number?", "options": ["The graph must start at 1", "Tickets must be even", "Slopes cannot be negative", "You cannot sell a negative or partial ticket"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.4: Linear Models in the Real World$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,2,0,3}'::int[], array[$j$30 + 10(4) = 70.$j$,$j$15x + 120 = 420 gives x = 20.$j$,$j$The slope is the rate of change per week.$j$,$j$Real situations impose limits on the inputs.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.4: Linear Models in the Real World$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 15, $j$Unit Quiz 3: Functions and Linear Models$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Find the slope through (0, 0) and (3, 6).", "options": ["1/2", "3", "2", "6"]}, {"q": "If f(x) = −2x + 5, what is f(3)?", "options": ["11", "−1", "−11", "1"]}, {"q": "What is the y intercept of y = 4x − 7?", "options": ["−7", "4", "−4", "7"]}, {"q": "Which is the equation of the line through (0, 3) with slope −1?", "options": ["y = x + 3", "y = x − 3", "y = −x + 3", "y = −3x + 1"]}, {"q": "A line is parallel to y = −2x + 1. What is its slope?", "options": ["−2", "2", "−1/2", "1/2"]}, {"q": "A car travels 90 miles in 1.5 hours. What is the average rate?", "options": ["91.5 miles per hour", "60 miles per hour", "135 miles per hour", "45 miles per hour"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 3: Functions and Linear Models$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,1,0,2,0,1}'::int[], array[$j$6/3 = 2.$j$,$j$−6 + 5 = −1.$j$,$j$The constant term is the y intercept.$j$,$j$Use y = mx + b with m = −1 and b = 3.$j$,$j$Parallel lines have equal slopes.$j$,$j$90 ÷ 1.5 = 60.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 3: Functions and Linear Models$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
